{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kw8VyD2ZSNgM9NHt5sGNbS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd107-dir\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/dir: List Directory Contents\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  dir is a GNU coreutils utility equivalent to \"ls -C -b\". It lists directory contents in\n6\t  multi-column format with non-printable characters escaped using C-style backslash sequences.\n7\t  macOS does not ship a dir command; the Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n8\t  reference binary (gdir, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations,\n9\t  verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n10\t\n11\t  dir shares the bulk of its logic with cmd/ls (srd008-ls). The implementation must invoke\n12\t  the same listing engine with hardcoded defaults for multi-column layout (-C) and C-style\n13\t  escaping (-b). dir requires pkg/sys for SIGPIPE handling and terminal width queries.\n14\t\n15\tgoals:\n16\t  - G1: List directory contents in multi-column format with C-style escaping of non-printable characters, matching GNU dir (ls -C -b) output.\n17\t  - G2: Accept the same arguments as ls, with the only difference being the default format and quoting style.\n18\t\n19\trequirements:\n20\t  R1:\n21\t    title: Default behavior and output format\n22\t    items:\n23\t      - R1.1:\n24\t          text: |\n25\t            Must list directory contents in multi-column format by default, regardless of\n26\t            whether stdout is a TTY. This matches GNU dir behavior where -C is the default\n27\t            format. When stdout is a TTY, terminal width is obtained by calling\n28\t            pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() (int, error). When stdout is not a TTY, must use a\n29\t            default width of 80 columns.\n30\t      - R1.2:\n31\t          text: |\n32\t            Must escape non-printable characters in filenames using C-style backslash\n33\t            sequences by default. This matches the -b (--escape) behavior of ls. Backslash,\n34\t            newline, tab, and other control characters are printed as \\\\, \\n, \\t, and \\NNN\n35\t            (octal) respectively. Spaces in filenames are printed as \\  (backslash space).\n36\t      - R1.3:\n37\t          text: |\n38\t            Must sort entries in the C locale order (LC_COLLATE=C) by default, matching\n39\t            ls default sort behavior. Entries whose names start with \".\" are excluded unless\n40\t            -a or -A is given.\n41\t      - R1.4:\n42\t          text: When no directory arguments are given, must default to the current directory (\".\").\n43\t      - R1.5:\n44\t          text: Must accept all flags that ls accepts. dir is identical to ls except for the default format (-C) and default quoting style (-b). All other behavior is inherited from ls.\n45\t\n46\t  R2:\n47\t    title: Exit codes and signal handling\n48\t    items:\n49\t      - R2.1:\n50\t          text: Must exit 0 when all listed paths are accessed and output is written successfully.\n51\t      - R2.2:\n52\t          text: Must exit 1 when a minor problem occurs (permission denied, file not found). Must print a diagnostic to stderr and continue processing remaining entries.\n53\t      - R2.3:\n54\t          text: Must exit 2 when a serious problem occurs such as an invalid command-line option, matching GNU dir (and ls) exit-code contract.\n55\t      - R2.4:\n56\t          text: Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup by calling pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() so that piping dir output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message.\n57\t\n58\tnon_goals:\n59\t  - dir does not add any flags beyond what ls provides; it is strictly ls with different defaults.\n60\t  - Color output (--color) is not enabled by default in dir; it must be explicitly requested.\n61\t  - Long format (-l) is not the default; -C is the default.\n62\t  - Recursive listing (-R) is supported but not default behavior.\n63\t\n64\tacceptance_criteria:\n65\t  - id: AC1\n66\t    criterion: \"Default mode comparison -- gdir and go run ./cmd/dir on a test directory produce identical multi-column output with escaped filenames. Differential tests must set LC_ALL=C.\"\n67\t    traces:\n68\t      - R1.1\n69\t      - R1.2\n70\t      - R1.3\n71\t      - R1.4\n72\t      - R1.5\n73\t  - id: AC2\n74\t    criterion: \"Non-TTY output -- gdir piped to cat and go run ./cmd/dir piped to cat produce identical 80-column multi-column output.\"\n75\t    traces:\n76\t      - R1.1\n77\t      - R1.2\n78\t  - id: AC3\n79\t    criterion: \"Escaping -- filenames containing spaces, backslashes, newlines, and control characters are escaped identically by gdir and the Go binary.\"\n80\t    traces:\n81\t      - R1.2\n82\t  - id: AC4\n83\t    criterion: \"Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given a non-existent path and exits 2 for an invalid option, matching gdir behavior.\"\n84\t    traces:\n85\t      - R2.1\n86\t      - R2.2\n87\t      - R2.3\n88\t      - R2.4\n89\t  - id: AC5\n90\t    criterion: \"cmd/dir compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n91\t    traces:\n92\t      - R1.1\n93\t      - R1.2\n94\t      - R1.3\n95\t      - R1.4\n96\t      - R1.5\n97\t      - R2.1\n98\t      - R2.2\n99\t      - R2.3\n100\t      - R2.4\n101\t\n102\tpackage_contract:\n103\t  imports:\n104\t    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/format\n105\t    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n106\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5eb3c899-5e95-42b9-988a-cff6c361234f","uuid":"572531e8-f43d-4858-9775-22ae175719cf","timestamp":"2026-05-27T19:25:46.770Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5606/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd107-dir.yaml","content":"id: srd107-dir\ntitle: \"cmd/dir: List Directory Contents\"\n\nproblem: |\n  dir is a GNU coreutils utility equivalent to \"ls -C -b\". It lists directory contents in\n  multi-column format with non-printable characters escaped using C-style backslash sequences.\n  macOS does not ship a dir command; the Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n  reference binary (gdir, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations,\n  verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\n  dir shares the bulk of its logic with cmd/ls (srd008-ls). The implementation must invoke\n  the same listing engine with hardcoded defaults for multi-column layout (-C) and C-style\n  escaping (-b). dir requires pkg/sys for SIGPIPE handling and terminal width queries.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: List directory contents in multi-column format with C-style escaping of non-printable characters, matching GNU dir (ls -C -b) output.\n  - G2: Accept the same arguments as ls, with the only difference being the default format and quoting style.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default behavior and output format\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: |\n            Must list directory contents in multi-column format by default, regardless of\n            whether stdout is a TTY. This matches GNU dir behavior where -C is the default\n            format. When stdout is a TTY, terminal width is obtained by calling\n            pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() (int, error). When stdout is not a TTY, must use a\n            default width of 80 columns.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: |\n            Must escape non-printable characters in filenames using C-style backslash\n            sequences by default. This matches the -b (--escape) behavior of ls. Backslash,\n            newline, tab, and other control characters are printed as \\\\, \\n, \\t, and \\NNN\n            (octal) respectively. Spaces in filenames are printed as \\  (backslash space).\n      - R1.3:\n          text: |\n            Must sort entries in the C locale order (LC_COLLATE=C) by default, matching\n            ls default sort behavior. Entries whose names start with \".\" are excluded unless\n            -a or -A is given.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: When no directory arguments are given, must default to the current directory (\".\").\n      - R1.5:\n          text: Must accept all flags that ls accepts. dir is identical to ls except for the default format (-C) and default quoting style (-b). All other behavior is inherited from ls.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Exit codes and signal handling\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when all listed paths are accessed and output is written successfully.\n      - R2.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when a minor problem occurs (permission denied, file not found). Must print a diagnostic to stderr and continue processing remaining entries.\n      - R2.3:\n          text: Must exit 2 when a serious problem occurs such as an invalid command-line option, matching GNU dir (and ls) exit-code contract.\n      - R2.4:\n          text: Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup by calling pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() so that piping dir output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - dir does not add any flags beyond what ls provides; it is strictly ls with different defaults.\n  - Color output (--color) is not enabled by default in dir; it must be explicitly requested.\n  - Long format (-l) is not the default; -C is the default.\n  - Recursive listing (-R) is supported but not default behavior.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"Default mode comparison -- gdir and go run ./cmd/dir on a test directory produce identical multi-column output with escaped filenames. Differential tests must set LC_ALL=C.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R1.5\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"Non-TTY output -- gdir piped to cat and go run ./cmd/dir piped to cat produce identical 80-column multi-column output.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"Escaping -- filenames containing spaces, backslashes, newlines, and control characters are escaped identically by gdir and the Go binary.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.2\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given a non-existent path and exits 2 for an invalid option, matching gdir behavior.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"cmd/dir compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R1.5\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n\npackage_contract:\n  imports:\n    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/format\n    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n","numLines":106,"startLine":1,"totalLines":106}}}
