{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user (cobbler orchestrator) has assigned Task #5606: implement `cmd/dir` utility covering srd107-dir requirements R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, and R1.4. This requires creating two files: `cmd/dir/main.go` (the dir implementation - list directory contents in multi-column format equivalent to `ls -C -b`) and `cmd/dir/dir_test.go` (differential tests against the `gdir` GNU reference binary). The task must follow the execution constitution's coding standards, shared protocols (differential testing, SIGPIPE handling, graceful skip), and not deviate from the specified files.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - `dir` is a GNU coreutils utility equivalent to `ls -C -b` (multi-column format with C-style backslash escaping)\n   - Differential testing pattern: build Go binary, find reference binary (`gdir`), run both with same inputs, compare stdout/stderr/exit code\n   - Multi-column layout using `pkg/format.Columns()` which fills top-to-bottom, left-to-right with 2-space gap\n   - Terminal width detection via `pkg/sys.TerminalWidth()` for TTY, default 80 columns for non-TTY\n   - C-style escaping: `\\\\` for backslash, `\\n` for newline, `\\t` for tab, `\\NNN` octal for other control chars, `\\ ` for spaces\n   - C locale sorting (LC_ALL=C) for consistent ordering\n   - SIGPIPE handler installation via `pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`\n   - cmd/ls has codestatus \"not started\" so dir must be standalone implementation (no ls code to reuse)\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd107-dir.yaml` (READ)\n     - This is the SRD defining dir's requirements. Key requirements for this task:\n     - R1.1: Multi-column format by default regardless of TTY status. Use `sys.TerminalWidth()` for TTY, 80 columns for non-TTY.\n     - R1.2: C-style backslash escaping of non-printable chars (`\\\\`, `\\n`, `\\t`, `\\NNN` octal, `\\ ` for spaces)\n     - R1.3: Sort entries in C locale order, exclude dot-files unless -a/-A given\n     - R1.4: Default to current directory \".\" when no directory arguments given\n     - R1.5: Accept all ls flags (NOT in task scope - task only covers R1.1-R1.4)\n     - R2.4: SIGPIPE handler requirement\n     - Non-goals: no color by default, no -l default\n   \n   - `pkg/sys/` (provided in context) - Provides `InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`, `TerminalWidth()`, `IsTerminal()`, `Stat()`, `Lstat()`\n   - `pkg/format/` (provided in context) - Provides `Columns()`, `PadRight()`, `PadLeft()`, `ColorEnabled()`, `FileTypeColor()`\n   - `pkg/testutils/` (provided in context) - Provides `BuildBinary()`, `RunDiffTests()`, `DiffTest` struct\n   \n   - `cmd/dir/main.go` - TO BE CREATED\n   - `cmd/dir/dir_test.go` - TO BE CREATED\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors yet - only one tool call was made (reading the SRD) before the summary was requested.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Key design challenge identified: cmd/ls has no existing implementation (codestatus \"not started\"), so dir must implement its own directory listing logic rather than delegating to a shared ls engine. The task scope is limited to R1.1-R1.4 (not R1.5 \"accept all ls flags\"), so the implementation can focus on core directory listing with multi-column output and C-style escaping without needing to support every ls flag.\n   - The `format.Columns()` function handles the multi-column grid layout (fills top-to-bottom, left-to-right with 2-space gap), which is exactly what dir needs for `-C` mode.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - Message 1: The massive task prompt containing full project context, task description (#5606), execution constitution, go style constitution, constraints, SRD references, required reading, files to create, requirements (R1.1-R1.4), design decisions (D1-D4), and acceptance criteria (AC1-AC4).\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Create `cmd/dir/main.go` implementing srd107 R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.4\n   - Create `cmd/dir/dir_test.go` with differential tests against `gdir`\n   - Verify compilation with `go build ./cmd/dir/`\n   - Verify tests pass with `go test ./cmd/dir/ -count=1`\n   - Verify `go vet ./cmd/dir/` reports no issues\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I had just finished reading the SRD file (`docs/specs/software-requirements/srd107-dir.yaml`) and was about to plan and implement the two source files. No code has been written yet. The SRD reading revealed that dir needs to:\n   - List directory contents in multi-column format (using `format.Columns()`)\n   - Escape non-printable characters C-style\n   - Sort in C locale order\n   - Default to \".\" when no args\n   - Install SIGPIPE handler\n   - Use terminal width for TTY, 80 for non-TTY\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Write both `cmd/dir/main.go` and `cmd/dir/dir_test.go` in a single pass, then compile and test. The implementation should:\n   - main.go: Parse args (defaulting to \".\"), read directory entries, sort them in C locale order, escape non-printable chars with C-style sequences, format into multi-column layout using `format.Columns()` with terminal width from `sys.TerminalWidth()` or 80 for non-TTY, print output.\n   - dir_test.go: Use `testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\")`, `exec.LookPath(\"gdir\")` with skip, and `testutils.RunDiffTests` with test cases covering each of R1.1-R1.4.\n   \n   Per the task description: \"requirements: R1: Implement srd107 requirement R1.1 per the SRD, R2: Implement srd107 requirement R1.2 per the SRD, R3: Implement srd107 requirement R1.3 per the SRD, R4: Implement srd107 requirement R1.4 per the SRD\"\n   \n   Per constraints: \"Complete all code changes before running `go build` or `go test`. Do not compile incrementally after each small edit.\"\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/pdiddy/.claude/projects/-private-var-folders-dl-cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn-T-go-unix-utils-worktrees-5606/5eb3c899-5e95-42b9-988a-cff6c361234f.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5eb3c899-5e95-42b9-988a-cff6c361234f","uuid":"275928bb-bf39-4c3e-afce-19c810f10fb7","timestamp":"2026-05-27T19:26:38.552Z","isSynthetic":true}
