` which correctly handles DEL (0x7f = 0177 in octal) and all high bytes, formatting them as three-digit octal escape sequences. Now I'm wondering about edge cases with the `?` character in certain GNU ls modes. are replaced with `?`. But in `-b` (escape) mode, they use octal sequences. My code correctly uses octal. And `?` is a regular printable character (0x3F) so it's not escaped. Good.\n\nWhat about `\"` (double quote, 0x22)? In C locale escape mode, double quotes are NOT escaped (only in `-Q`/`--quote-name` mode). Single quotes? Also not escaped in `-b` mode. Good.\n\nWhat about `'` (single quote)? GNU ls -b does NOT escape single quotes. My code doesn't either. Good.\n\nWhat about `!`, `#`, `$`, etc.? These are regular printable ASCII characters and are NOT escaped. Correct.\n\nOne more: the `\\0` (null byte). This technically can't appear in a filename on Unix, so we don't need to handle it. But if it somehow did, my code would output `\\000`. That's fine.\n\nI think my escaping logic is correct. Now I'm ready to write the full implementation. I could optimize by pre-computing octal strings, but using string building with direct writes is simpler and sufficient for correctness. I'm going to write the escaping function using a string builder and handling each character case.\n\nWriting escape function... I'm reconsidering the approach to escape sequences—using `fmt.Fprintf` with an octal format specifier would be cleaner than manually constructing the escape codes. But since I still need `fmt` for output anyway, it doesn't really reduce imports. Now I'm thinking about buffering for performance, since writing unbuffered to stdout for large directories could be slow. I should use `bufio.NewWriter` to buffer output and flush at the end, though I need to be careful about error handling and ensuring the buffer gets flushed properly.\n\nStructuring the main function to pass a buffered writer through to the run function...\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering—stderr doesn't