before displaying. Currently my code sorts them using Go's string sorting, which matches C locale behavior. That should be correct.\n\nBut I'm realizing there's a subtlety with how GNU ls handles arguments — it sorts all arguments together regardless of whether they're files or directories, then groups the output by type. My current approach of separating files and directories into different slices means the directory arguments aren't being sorted, which doesn't match GNU behavior. files first, directories second. But within each group, the original sort applies.\n\nWait, actually I think GNU ls sorts file arguments and directory arguments separately. Let me check:\n\n```\n$ ls dir2 dir1\ndir1:\n...\n\ndir2:\n...\n```\n\nYes, directory arguments are sorted. And file arguments are sorted. So I need to sort both:\n\n```go\nsort.Strings(files)\nsort.Strings(dirs)\n```\n\nLet me add `sort.Strings(dirs)` to my code. Actually, looking more carefully, GNU ls processes args in this order:\n1. Sort all arguments\n2. Process non-directories first (files)\n3. Process directories\n\nBut in my code, I separate files and dirs first, then sort each group. The result should be the same since sorting a group independently = sorting together then separating.\n\nWait, not exactly. If I have args [\"z-dir\", \"a-file\", \"m-dir\", \"b-file\"], GNU ls would sort to [\"a-file\", \"b-file\", \"m-dir\", \"z-dir\"], then output files first, then directories. My code separates into files=[\"a-file\", \"b-file\"] and dirs=[\"z-dir\", \"m-dir\"], then sorts each: files=[\"a-file\", \"b-file\"], dirs=[\"m-dir\", \"z-dir\"]. Same result. Good.\n\nNow I need to finalize the code. One more thing to check: does my code handle symlinks to directories correctly? Since I'm using `os.Stat()` which follows symlinks, `info.IsDir()` will return true for symlinks pointing to directories, so those get processed as directories.\n\nFor broken symlinks, `os.Stat()` fails, which triggers the error handling path