e 2-space separator, then \"mn\" with no padding since it's last on the row, giving \"gh      mn\". The algorithm checks out.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the layout when there are 4 columns but the last column is completely empty—GNU ls would still attempt 4 columns since the layout fits, and the empty column doesn't affect the total width calculation. Let me trace through the computeLayout logic to see how it handles columns with no entries.\n\nExamining the column width calculation... So with 4 columns, the empty fourth column still requires a separator, pushing the total width to 22, which exceeds the limit. But with 3 columns and 2 rows, the layout fits perfectly at exactly 20 width. My algorithm is correctly accounting for the separator costs between columns. rejects ncols=4 because the separator takes up 2 extra columns even though col 3 is empty. But GNU ls is smarter—it only counts columns that actually contain entries. With 5 entries and 4 columns, nrows=2 gives col 0 and 1 two entries each, col 2 one entry, and col 3 nothing. My algorithm naturally prefers ncols=3 since col 4 would be empty and waste a separator, and both ncols=3 and ncols=4 produce the same nrows anyway. Let me check what happens with ncols=5... ncols=4 gives 22 characters, which exceeds the 20-character limit, so that's rejected too. ncols=3 fits perfectly at exactly 20 characters across 2 rows, matching what GNU ls would produce. I should also handle the edge case where a single entry is wider than the terminal—in that case, the algorithm forces ncols=1 and lets entries wrap. Now I need to verify the error message format matches GNU dir's output for non-existent paths.\n\nDefining the program name normalizer...\n\nThe normalizer replaces \"gdir:\" with \"dir:\" in the output, so both the reference binary and my implementation produce matching error messages after normalization. I'm checking whether the error message format itself might differ between GNU dir and my implementation, which co