ond uses `(c>>3)&7` for 0-7, and the third uses `c&7` for 0-7. Each maps correctly to ASCII digit characters.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the implementation. But first, let me double-check the escape conditions: my fast-path check correctly identifies characters that need escaping (backslash, space, control characters, and non-ASCII), while printable ASCII like single and double quotes pass through unescaped, matching GNU ls behavior.\n\nOne concern with the test is whether creating files with special characters—spaces, backslashes, tabs—will work reliably on macOS, since filepath.Join just concatenates paths with forward slashes and the resulting filenames need to be valid on the filesystem.\n\nFor the backslash case, the filename on disk will literally contain a backslash character (which is valid on macOS), and both gdir and my binary should escape it the same way when listing. Same with spaces and tabs—they should be handled consistently by both implementations.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the octal escape formatting would produce the right output...\n\nthe manual bit manipulation approach I'm using matches what `fmt.Fprintf` with `%03o` would generate, so that's consistent. Let me verify I have all the necessary imports for the main file—I need errors, fmt, os, sort, and strings, plus the custom sys package, and I'm using all of them.\n\nFor the test file, I'm checking the imports there too—bytes, os, os/exec, path/filepath, testing, and the testutils package.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3436,"cache_read_input_tokens":29212,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3436},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5eb3c899-5e95-42b9-988a-cff6c361234f","uuid":"88436b69-6ce8-4ede-8caf-cddac4b6c6fe"}
